<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ore Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Erzgebirge, Krušné hory</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ore Mountains, in German Erzgebirge and in Czech Krušné hory, are a mountain range along the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> strikes from the 12th century at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg">Freiberg</a> and from the 1470s at Schneeberg, Annaberg and Marienberg made Saxony one of the richest states in the Empire, and silver from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jachymov">Joachimsthal</a> on the Bohemian side gave its name to the thaler. The mining landscape is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ore-mountains.jpg" alt="Ore Mountains" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rafael Brix · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myslivny_bozi_dar_lake.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
